---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import { SITE } from '../../data/site'

const toc = [
  { id: 'requirements', label: 'requirements' },
  { id: 'install', label: 'install from the release' },
  { id: 'arch-linux', label: 'arch linux (aur)' },
  { id: 'verify', label: 'verify the install' },
  { id: 'update', label: 'update' },
  { id: 'troubleshooting', label: 'troubleshooting' },
]
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Install openclaude — GitHub Releases, macOS, Linux & Windows"
  description="How to install the openclaude CLI from the GitHub Releases .tgz on macOS, Linux, and Windows. Requirements, global install, verifying the install, and updating to the latest version."
  heading="install openclaude"
  lede="Download the .tgz from GitHub Releases on macOS, Linux, or Windows. An AUR package exists for Arch."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="requirements">requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Node.js 22 or newer</strong> — grab it from <a href="https://nodejs.org/" rel="noopener">nodejs.org</a> and confirm with <code>node --version</code>.</li>
    <li>A terminal: Terminal/iTerm on macOS, any shell on Linux, PowerShell or Windows Terminal on Windows.</li>
    <li><code>ripgrep</code> is bundled; if startup reports <code>ripgrep not found</code>, install it system-wide and confirm <code>rg --version</code> works.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="install">install from the release</h2>
  <p>
    Every release publishes <code>openclaude-&lt;version&gt;.tgz</code>, plus an
    <code>openclaude-latest.tgz</code> alias that always points at the newest one:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{SITE.installCommand}</code></pre>
  <p>On Windows PowerShell:</p>
  <pre><code>Invoke-WebRequest -OutFile openclaude.tgz {SITE.releasesUrl}/latest/download/openclaude-latest.tgz
npm install -g .\openclaude.tgz</code></pre>
  <p>
    Prefer downloading by hand? Grab <code>openclaude-&lt;version&gt;.tgz</code> from the
    <a href={`${SITE.releasesUrl}/latest`} rel="noopener">latest release</a> and install it by path:
  </p>
  <pre><code>npm install -g ./openclaude-&lt;version&gt;.tgz</code></pre>
  <p>
    This installs the <code>openclaude</code> binary globally. On macOS/Linux you may need
    a Node installation that doesn't require <code>sudo</code> for global installs (nvm,
    fnm, or Homebrew node all work).
  </p>

  <h2 id="arch-linux">arch linux (aur)</h2>
  <p>
    A community-maintained <a href="https://aur.archlinux.org/packages/openclaude" rel="noopener">AUR package</a> is available:
  </p>
  <pre><code>paru -S openclaude</code></pre>

  <h2 id="verify">verify the install</h2>
  <pre><code>openclaude --version</code></pre>
  <p>
    To check which version is the latest published release, open the
    <a href={`${SITE.releasesUrl}/latest`} rel="noopener">Releases page</a> — the newest
    tag is <code>v&lt;version&gt;</code>, and the version printed by the CLI must match it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="update">update</h2>
  <p>
    Download and reinstall the newest <code>.tgz</code> — <code>openclaude-latest.tgz</code>
    always resolves to the newest release:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{SITE.installCommand}</code></pre>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Old version after update</strong> — your shell may cache the binary path; run <code>hash -r</code> (bash/zsh) or open a new terminal.</li>
    <li><strong>Multiple Node versions</strong> — global installs are per Node version with nvm/fnm; make sure you installed under the version your shell uses.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything else</strong> — run <code>/doctor</code> inside openclaude to diagnose the installation, or check the <a href={`${SITE.github}/issues`} rel="noopener">issue tracker</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Next: <a href="/docs/quickstart/">run your first session →</a></p>
</DocsLayout>